mooth.\\nFix \\\\(q\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^m\\\\) with \\\\(|q|=1\\\\). Assume:\\n\\n1. \\\\(|u(x,t)|=1\\\\) for all \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(t>0\\\\).\\n2. The acceleration \\\\(u_{tt}-\\\\Delta_xu\\\\) is normal to the sphere at\\n   \\\\(u\\\\): at each point there is a real scalar \\\\(a(x,t)\\\\) with\\n   \\\\(u_{tt}-\\\\Delta_xu=a u\\\\). No regularity of the selected scalar is\\n   assumed.\\n3. For every \\\\(0<s<t<\\\\infty\\\\), a compact set\\n   \\\\(C_{[s,t]}\\\\subset\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\) exists such that \\\\(u(x,r)=q\\\\) for\\n   \\\\(x\\\\notin C_{[s,t]}\\\\), \\\\(r\\\\in[s,t]\\\\).\\n\\nWith Euclidean norms and Lebesgue measure, define\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne(x,t)=|u_t(x,t)|^2+\\\\sum_{j=1}^n|\\\\partial_{x_j}u(x,t)|^2,\\n\\\\qquad E(t)=\\\\int_{\\\\mathbb R^n}e(x,t)\\\\,dx.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe spatial gradient square is the Frobenius square, and\\n\\\\(\\\\Delta_xu=\\\\sum_j\\\\partial_{x_jx_j}u\\\\) component by component.\\nProve integrability of \\\\(e(\\\\cdot,t)\\\\), differentiability of \\\\(E\\\\),\\nand \\\\(E'(t)=0\\\\) for every \\\\(t>0\\\\). No assertion at time zero is required.\",\"conclusion\":\"For every t>0, e(·,t) is Lebesgue integrable on ℝ^n. The finite energy E:(0,∞)→ℝ is differentiable at every t>0 and E'(t)=0. In particular E(b)=E(a) for all a,b>0, with exactly the normalization E(t)=∫_{ℝ^n} (|u_t(x,t)|²+∑_{j=1}^n |∂_{x_j}u(x,t)|²) dx and no assertion at time zero.\",\"parameters\":[{\"symbol\":\"n\",\"type\":\"natural number\"},{\"symbol\":\"m\",\"type\":\"natural number\"},{\"symbol\":\"u\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n×(0,∞)→ℝ^m\"},{\"symbol\":\"q\",\"type\":\"vector in ℝ^m\"}],\"hypotheses\":[{\"id\":\"H001\",\"statement\":\"n≥1 and m≥1.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:25\"},{\"id\":\"H002\",\"statement\":\"u is jointly smooth on the open set ℝ^n×(0,∞).\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:25-26\"},{\"id\":\"H003\",\"statement\":\"|q|=1.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:27\"},{\"id\":\"H004\",\"statement\":\"For every x∈ℝ^n and t>0, |u(x,t)|=1.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:29\"},{\"id\":\"H005\",\"statement\":\"For every x∈ℝ^n and t>0 there exists a real scalar a such that u_tt(x,t)−Δ_xu(x,t)=a u(x,t). No regularity of a selection of these scalars is assumed.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:30-33\"},{\"id\":\"H006\",\"statement\":\"For every pair of real times 0<s<t<∞ there exists a compact set C⊂ℝ^n such that u(x,r)=q for every x∉C and every r∈[s,t].\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:34-36\"}],\"definitions\":[{\"id\":\"D001\",\"symbol\":\"∂\",\"type\":\"coordinate differentiation and Euclidean notation convention on the given open domain\",\"definition\":\"On ℝ^n and ℝ^m use the standard Euclidean inner product v·w=∑_k v_k w_k and norm |v|=(v·v)^(1/2). Spatial Lebesgue measure dx is the product of the n ordinary one-dimensional Lebesgue measures in the standard coordinates. For any function on ℝ^n×(0,∞), ∂_{x_j} means its componentwise derivative in spatial coordinate j (1≤j≤n), and ∂_t its componentwise time derivative, where these derivatives exist. In particular u_t=∂_t u, u_tt=∂_t(∂_t u), and ∂_{x_jx_j}u=∂_{x_j}(∂_{x_j}u). For scalar functions the same conventions apply. D_xu denotes the matrix with columns ∂_{x_j}u, and its squared Frobenius norm is ∑_{j=1}^n |∂_{x_j}u|². Smooth means derivatives of every finite order exist and are continuous on the stated open domain. An integral without a displayed measure is the Lebesgue integral; integrable means measurable with finite integral of the absolute value (or norm).\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"v\",\"type\":\"vector in the relevant Euclidean space\"},{\"symbol\":\"w\",\"type\":\"vector in the same Euclidean space\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"spatial index with 1≤j≤n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\",\"u\",\"v\",\"w\",\"j\"]},{\"id\":\"D002\",\"symbol\":\"Δ_xu\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n×(0,∞)→ℝ^m, when the stated derivatives exist\",\"definition\":\"For every x∈ℝ^n and t>0, Δ_xu(x,t)=∑_{j=1}^n ∂_{x_jx_j}u(x,t), component by component.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D001\"],\"requires\":[\"H002\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"x\",\"t\"]},{\"id\":\"D003\",\"symbol\":\"e\",\"type\":\"real-valued function on ℝ^n×(0,∞)\",\"definition\":{\"from_task\":\"problem.md\",\"start_line\":40,\"end_line\":43},\"depends_on\":[\"D001\"],\"requires\":[\"H002\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"spatial index 1≤j≤n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"x\",\"t\",\"j\"]},{\"id\":\"D004\",\"symbol\":\"E\",\"type\":\"real-valued function on (0,∞)\",\"definition\":\"After integrability has been established, define E(t)=∫_{ℝ^n} e(x,t) dx for every t>0. Differentiability and E'(t) mean the ordinary two-sided real derivative on this open time interval, equivalently the derivative of any local extension near t.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D003\"],\"requires\":[\"N002\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"e\",\"x\",\"t\"]}],\"logic\":{\"classical\":true,\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\",\"implication_introduction\"]},\"pins\":{},\"inputs\":[]},\"graph\":{\"format_version\":2,\"case_id\":\"wave-map-energy\",\"spec_sha256\":\"controller-bound\",\"propositions\":[{\"id\":\"H001\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"H002\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\"],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"H003\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\"],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"q\"]},{\"id\":\"H004\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"x\",\"t\"]},{\"id\":\"H005\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real scalar, existentially quantified at each point\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"Δ_xu\",\"x\",\"t\",\"a\"]},{\"id\":\"H006\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"s\",\"type\":\"real time\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real time\"},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n, existential after s,t\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"real time\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"s\",\"t\",\"C\",\"x\",\"r\"]},{\"id\":\"M001\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let A be a measurable space with a measure μ, and G a normed additive commutative group and real normed vector space. Let I be an index type and l a countably generated filter on I. Given F:I→A→G, f:A→G and b:A→ℝ, assume (i) eventually in l, F(i) is μ-a.e. strongly measurable; (ii) eventually in l, |F(i,x)|≤b(x) for μ-a.e. x; (iii) b is μ-integrable; (iv) for μ-a.e. x, F(i,x) tends along l to f(x). Then ∫ F(i,x) dμ tends along l to ∫ f(x) dμ.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"measure on A\"},{\"symbol\":\"G\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and normed space over ℝ\"},{\"symbol\":\"I\",\"type\":\"index type\"},{\"symbol\":\"l\",\"type\":\"filter on I\"},{\"symbol\":\"F\",\"type\":\"map I→A→G\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map A→G\"},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"map A→ℝ\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"μ\",\"G\",\"I\",\"l\",\"F\",\"f\",\"b\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.tendsto_integral_filter_of_dominated_convergence\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A has a measurable-space structure; μ is a measure; G has a normed additive commutative group and real normed-space structure.\",\"l is a countably generated filter.\",\"Eventually F(i) is μ-a.e. strongly measurable.\",\"Eventually |F(i,x)|≤b(x) for μ-a.e. x.\",\"b is μ-integrable.\",\"For μ-a.e. x, F(i,x) tends along l to f(x).\"]}},{\"id\":\"M002\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let V be a complete normed additive commutative group and normed vector space over ℝ, let α,β∈ℝ, and let f:ℝ→V. If f is differentiable at every point of the unordered closed interval between α and β and its ordinary derivative deriv f is interval-integrable for Lebesgue measure between α and β, then ∫_{α}^{β} deriv f(r) dr=f(β)−f(α), with oriented interval integral.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"complete real normed vector space\"},{\"symbol\":\"α\",\"type\":\"real\"},{\"symbol\":\"β\",\"type\":\"real\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→V\"}],\"objects\":[\"V\",\"α\",\"β\",\"f\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"intervalIntegral.integral_deriv_eq_sub\",\"hypotheses\":[\"V is a normed additive commutative group, a real normed space, and complete.\",\"f is differentiable at every point of the unordered closed interval between α and β.\",\"deriv f is interval-integrable with respect to Lebesgue measure between α and β.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M003\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let A,B be measurable spaces, μ,ν respectively s-finite measures on them, and V a normed additive commutative group and real normed vector space. For every f:A×B→V integrable for μ×ν, ∫ f(z) d(μ×ν)(z)=∫_A (∫_B f(x,y) dν(y)) dμ(x).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"B\",\"type\":\"measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"s-finite measure on A\"},{\"symbol\":\"ν\",\"type\":\"s-finite measure on B\"},{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"real normed vector space with normed additive commutative group\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map A×B→V\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"B\",\"μ\",\"ν\",\"V\",\"f\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.integral_prod\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A,B are measurable spaces; μ,ν are s-finite measures; V is a normed additive commutative group and real normed space.\",\"f is integrable for the product measure μ×ν.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M004\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let K be an RCLike field (ℝ or ℂ with its standard real-algebra, conjugation and norm structure), V a normed additive commutative group and normed space over K, f:K→V, S⊂K, ξ,ζ∈S and c∈ℝ. If f is differentiable over K at every point of S, |deriv f(z)|≤c for every z∈S, and S is real-convex, then |f(ζ)−f(ξ)|≤c|ζ−ξ|.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"RCLike field\"},{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and normed space over K\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map K→V\"},{\"symbol\":\"S\",\"type\":\"subset of K\"},{\"symbol\":\"ξ\",\"type\":\"point of K\"},{\"symbol\":\"ζ\",\"type\":\"point of K\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"real\"}],\"objects\":[\"K\",\"V\",\"f\",\"S\",\"ξ\",\"ζ\",\"c\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_deriv_le\",\"hypotheses\":[\"K is an RCLike field; V is a normed additive commutative group and normed K-space.\",\"f is differentiable over K at every point of S.\",\"For every z∈S, |deriv f(z)|≤c.\",\"S is real-convex.\",\"ξ∈S and ζ∈S.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M005\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let X carry a measurable space and a topology such that open sets are measurable. Let μ be a measure finite on compact sets of X. Let V be a normed additive commutative group, and f:X→V. If f is continuous and has compact support (the closure of {x:f(x)≠0} is compact), then f is μ-integrable.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"X\",\"type\":\"topological measurable space with measurable opens\"},{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"measure finite on compact sets of X\"},{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map X→V\"}],\"objects\":[\"X\",\"μ\",\"V\",\"f\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Continuous.integrable_of_hasCompactSupport\",\"hypotheses\":[\"X has measurable and topological structures with measurable opens; V is a normed additive commutative group; μ is finite on compact sets.\",\"f is continuous.\",\"The closure of the nonzero set of f is compact.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M006\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let L be a nonempty linearly ordered topological space in which every upper closed ray is closed, Y a topological space, K⊂Y compact, and f:Y→L continuous on K. Then f(K) is bounded above: there is b∈L with f(y)≤b for every y∈K.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"L\",\"type\":\"nonempty linearly ordered topological space with closed upper rays\"},{\"symbol\":\"Y\",\"type\":\"topological space\"},{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"subset of Y\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map Y→L\"},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"element of L, existential in conclusion\"}],\"objects\":[\"L\",\"Y\",\"K\",\"f\",\"b\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"IsCompact.bddAbove_image\",\"hypotheses\":[\"L is a nonempty linearly ordered topological space with closed upper rays, and Y is a topological space.\",\"K is compact.\",\"f is continuous on K.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M007\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let K be an RCLike field, V a normed additive commutative group, an inner-product space over K and also a normed space over ℝ. Let f,g:ℝ→V, v,w∈V and r∈ℝ. If f has real derivative v at r and g has real derivative w at r, then t↦⟨f(t),g(t)⟩_K has real derivative ⟨f(r),w⟩_K+⟨v,g(r)⟩_K at r.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"RCLike field\"},{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group, inner-product space over K, and real normed space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→V\"},{\"symbol\":\"g\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→V\"},{\"symbol\":\"v\",\"type\":\"vector in V\"},{\"symbol\":\"w\",\"type\":\"vector in V\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"real\"}],\"objects\":[\"K\",\"V\",\"f\",\"g\",\"v\",\"w\",\"r\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"HasDerivAt.inner\",\"hypotheses\":[\"K is RCLike; V is a normed additive commutative group, an inner-product space over K, and a real normed space.\",\"f has real derivative v at r.\",\"g has real derivative w at r.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M008\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let K be a nontrivially normed field, V,W normed additive commutative groups and normed K-spaces, f:V→W, z∈V, and k∈ℕ∞ω (extended smoothness order, including infinity and analytic order). If f is ContDiffAt K k at z and minSmoothness K 2≤k, then its second Fréchet derivative is symmetric at z: for every v,w∈V, (D(Df)(z)v)w=(D(Df)(z)w)v. Here D is the native Fréchet derivative (zero where no derivative exists), and minSmoothness K 2 is 2 for RCLike normed fields and analytic order otherwise.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"nontrivially normed field\"},{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and normed K-space\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and normed K-space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map V→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"point of V\"},{\"symbol\":\"k\",\"type\":\"extended smoothness order ℕ∞ω\"},{\"symbol\":\"v\",\"type\":\"vector in V\"},{\"symbol\":\"w\",\"type\":\"vector in V\"}],\"objects\":[\"K\",\"V\",\"W\",\"f\",\"z\",\"k\",\"v\",\"w\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"ContDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt\",\"hypotheses\":[\"K is a nontrivially normed field; V,W are normed additive commutative groups and normed K-spaces.\",\"f is ContDiffAt K k at z.\",\"minSmoothness K 2≤k.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M009\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let A be a measurable space, μ a measure on A, S⊂A, and V a topological space with an extended-seminormed additive-monoid structure. For c∈V, if μ(S)≠∞ and its extended norm |c|ₑ≠∞, then the constant function with value c is integrable on S for μ (that is, integrable for μ restricted to S).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"measure on A\"},{\"symbol\":\"S\",\"type\":\"subset of A\"},{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"topological extended-seminormed additive monoid\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"element of V\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"μ\",\"S\",\"V\",\"c\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.integrableOn_const\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A is a measurable space, μ a measure, and V has topology and extended-seminormed additive-monoid structure.\",\"μ(S)≠∞.\",\"The extended norm of c is finite.\"]}},{\"id\":\"N001\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"For all x∈ℝ^n and t>0, u(x,t)·u_t(x,t)=0 and u_t(x,t)·(u_tt(x,t)−Δ_xu(x,t))=0. Any real scalar a satisfying u_tt−Δ_xu=a u at that point equals ∑_{j=1}^n |∂_{x_j}u|²−|u_t|². The density e is smooth on ℝ^n×(0,∞), the mixed derivatives satisfy ∂_t∂_{x_j}u=∂_{x_j}u_t for every 1≤j≤n, and e_t(x,t)=2u_t(x,t)·u_tt(x,t)+2∑_{j=1}^n ∂_{x_j}u(x,t)·∂_{x_j}u_t(x,t). In particular this displayed e_t is continuous.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"index 1≤j≤n\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real scalar in the conditional scalar identity\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"Δ_xu\",\"e\",\"x\",\"t\",\"j\",\"a\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:52-68 and 89-94; calculus identities exported for the differentiation step.\"},{\"id\":\"N002\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"For every t>0, e(·,t) is integrable. More precisely, for each t0>0 there exist a compact C⊂ℝ^n, R>0 and B≥0 such that, writing J=[t0/2,3t0/2] and Q=[−R,R]^n, one has C⊂(−R,R)^n, u(x,r)=q for x∉C and r∈J, and all positive-order derivatives of u vanish for x∉C and r∈int J. The box Q has Lebesgue measure (2R)^n<∞. For every r∈int J, each of the spatial functions e, e_t, u_t·u_tt, ∂_{x_j}u·∂_{x_j}u_t, u_t·∂_{x_jx_j}u, u_t·∂_{x_j}u, and ∂_{x_j}(u_t·∂_{x_j}u), evaluated at time r, is continuous, vanishes outside C, and is integrable on ℝ^n, for each 1≤j≤n. Finally |e_t(x,r)|≤B for all (x,r)∈Q×[3t0/4,5t0/4].\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D003\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real\"},{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real\"},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"B\",\"type\":\"nonnegative real, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"J\",\"type\":\"time interval defined in statement\"},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"spatial box defined in statement\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"real time in stated ranges\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"spatial index 1≤j≤n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"t\",\"t0\",\"C\",\"R\",\"B\",\"J\",\"Q\",\"x\",\"r\",\"j\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:70-85 and 96-99; common-support and uniform-bound interface.\"},{\"id\":\"N003\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"For every t0>0, E is differentiable at t0 and E'(t0)=∫_{ℝ^n} e_t(x,t0) dx, where e_t(x,t0)=2u_t(x,t0)·u_tt(x,t0)+2∑_{j=1}^n ∂_{x_j}u(x,t0)·∂_{x_j}u_t(x,t0). All terms in this equality are finite integrals.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D003\",\"D004\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"E\",\"t0\",\"x\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:87-117; differentiation of the actual finite integral by dominated difference quotients.\"},{\"id\":\"N004\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"For every t0>0 and 1≤j≤n, ∫_{ℝ^n} ∂_{x_j}u(x,t0)·∂_{x_j}u_t(x,t0) dx=−∫_{ℝ^n} u_t(x,t0)·∂_{x_jx_j}u(x,t0) dx; both integrals are finite.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"index 1≤j≤n\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"t0\",\"j\",\"x\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:119-142; integration by parts via one-dimensional FTC and Fubini.\"},{\"id\":\"P\",\"kind\":\"target\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D003\",\"D004\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"positive real\"},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"positive real\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"E\",\"t\",\"a\",\"b\"]}],\"inferences\":[{\"id\":\"R001\",\"inputs\":[\"H002\",\"H004\",\"H005\",\"M007\",\"M008\"],\"output\":\"N001\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R001/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary x∈ℝ^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R001/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary t>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real scalar\",\"scope\":\"R001/S002\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S002\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose a real a with u_tt(x,t)−Δ_xu(x,t)=a u(x,t) by H005.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"H005\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"∂\",\"Δ_xu\",\"x\",\"t\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix x∈ℝ^n and t>0. By H002 all the coordinate curves and derivatives used here are differentiable near this interior point. H004 gives u(x,r)·u(x,r)=1 for r near t. Apply M007 over ℝ with both curves r↦u(x,r) and both derivatives u_t(x,t). The derivative of the constant right side is zero, while that of the left side is 2u(x,t)·u_t(x,t). Thus u·u_t=0 at (x,t). The same argument along any spatial coordinate gives u·∂_{x_j}u=0. These identities hold throughout the open domain.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"H004\",\"M007\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"x\",\"t\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"Throughout ℝ^n×(0,∞), u·u_t=0 and u·∂_{x_j}u=0 for each 1≤j≤n.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"spatial index 1≤j≤n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"j\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose a from H005 at (x,t). Using F001, u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu)=u_t·(a u)=a(u_t·u)=0, without differentiating a. For completeness retain the explicit equation from the supplied proof. Differentiate the identities u·u_t=0 and u·∂_{x_j}u=0 once more along their respective coordinate curves. M007 gives u·u_tt=−|u_t|² and u·∂_{x_jx_j}u=−|∂_{x_j}u|². Dotting u_tt−Δ_xu=a u with u and using H004 gives a=∑_j|∂_{x_j}u|²−|u_t|². The same algebra applies to every scalar satisfying the normality equation, not just the chosen witness.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"H004\",\"H005\",\"M007\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"Δ_xu\",\"x\",\"t\",\"a\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"At the arbitrary (x,t), u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu)=0, and every real scalar satisfying u_tt−Δ_xu=a u equals ∑_{j=1}^n|∂_{x_j}u|²−|u_t|².\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"Δ_xu\",\"x\",\"t\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"By H002, derivatives of every order are continuous; finite inner products and finite sums of their components are smooth, so e is smooth. To identify its time derivative, apply M008 over ℝ to u locally at (x,t), with order 2. One may extend u arbitrarily outside the open domain: all derivative statements are local and the point has a neighborhood wholly in the domain. Evaluate symmetry on the time direction and the jth spatial coordinate direction. These evaluations of the second Fréchet derivative are respectively ∂_t∂_{x_j}u and ∂_{x_j}u_t, hence they agree. Apply M007 to the time curves u_t and u_t, and to ∂_{x_j}u and itself for each index; differentiating the finite sum defining e now gives e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2∑_j∂_{x_j}u·∂_{x_j}u_t. It is continuous by H002. F001 and F002 and these identities hold for arbitrary x,t; universal introduction proves the full N001.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"M007\",\"M008\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"Δ_xu\",\"e\",\"x\",\"t\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"id\":\"A001\",\"input\":\"M007\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ, V=ℝ^m; f=g is the time curve r↦u(x,r), or the spatial-coordinate curve through (x,t), with v=w the corresponding coordinate derivative.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The standard Euclidean real inner-product and normed-space structures satisfy all the source structures.\",\"H002 supplies the first derivative of the chosen coordinate curve at the point.\",\"The second curve is the same curve and has the same derivative by H002.\"]},{\"id\":\"A002\",\"input\":\"M007\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ, V=ℝ^m. For the time identity use f(r)=u(x,r), g(r)=u_t(x,r); for spatial index j use the coordinate curves u and ∂_{x_j}u.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Standard real Euclidean structures apply.\",\"H002 gives derivative u_t, respectively ∂_{x_j}u, of the first curve.\",\"H002 gives derivative u_tt, respectively ∂_{x_jx_j}u, of the second curve; F001 supplies the identically zero inner product to be differentiated.\"]},{\"id\":\"A003\",\"input\":\"M008\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ, V=ℝ^n×ℝ, W=ℝ^m, z=(x,t), k=2, f a local representative of u extended off its domain; evaluate symmetry on the time and each spatial coordinate direction.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℝ is a nontrivially normed field; the finite-dimensional Euclidean product and codomain are normed real vector spaces.\",\"H002 gives C² at (x,t); a neighborhood is contained in ℝ^n×(0,∞), so any off-domain extension has this same germ.\",\"For ℝ, minSmoothness ℝ 2=2, and 2≤2.\"]},{\"id\":\"A004\",\"input\":\"M007\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ, V=ℝ^m. In the time variable take f=g=u_t(x,·), and f=g=∂_{x_j}u(x,·) for each j.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Standard real Euclidean inner-product structures apply.\",\"H002 gives derivatives u_tt and ∂_t∂_{x_j}u of the first curves.\",\"The second curves coincide with the first; M008 as just applied identifies ∂_t∂_{x_j}u with ∂_{x_j}u_t.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R002\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"H006\",\"M005\",\"M006\"],\"output\":\"N002\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D003\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S006\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix t0>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"J\",\"type\":\"closed real interval\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S006\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Put J=[t0/2,3t0/2].\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S006\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose the compact C asserted by F001.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"J\"]},{\"symbol\":\"L\",\"type\":\"real number\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S006\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose L with |x|≤L on C as asserted by F002.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"C\"]},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S006\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set R=max(1,L+1).\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"L\"]},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"closed box in ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S006\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set Q=[−R,R]^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"B0\",\"type\":\"real number\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S006\",\"end\":\"S006\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose B0 bounding |e_t| on the space-time box as in F004.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F004\"],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"Q\",\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"B\",\"type\":\"nonnegative real\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S006\",\"end\":\"S006\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set B=max(0,B0).\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"B0\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix t0>0 and put J=[t0/2,3t0/2]. Both endpoints are positive and the left is strictly smaller than the right. H006 therefore supplies a compact spatial set on which the perturbation may be supported throughout J.\",\"uses\":[\"H006\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"t0\",\"J\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"There exists a compact C⊂ℝ^n such that u(x,r)=q for every x∉C and every r∈J.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"real in J\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"J\",\"C\",\"x\",\"r\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose C as in F001. Apply M006 with real codomain to the continuous Euclidean norm on C. It gives a real upper bound for |x| on C, including when C is empty.\",\"uses\":[\"M006\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"C\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"There exists L∈ℝ such that |x|≤L for every x∈C.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"L\",\"type\":\"real\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of C\"}],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"L\",\"x\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Choose L from F002 and set R=max(1,L+1), Q=[−R,R]^n. Then R>0 and for x∈C every coordinate satisfies |x_j|≤|x|≤L<R. Thus C⊂(−R,R)^n=int Q. H001 ensures the stated positive finite dimensions; Q is a compact product of closed bounded intervals. For the coordinate product Lebesgue measure its measure is the finite product ∏_{j=1}^n(2R)=(2R)^n<∞.\",\"uses\":[\"H001\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\",\"C\",\"L\",\"R\",\"Q\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F003\",\"statement\":\"R>0, C⊂int Q=(−R,R)^n, Q is compact and its Lebesgue measure is (2R)^n<∞; u=q outside C throughout J.\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"J\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S004\",\"text\":\"The set (ℝ^n\\\\C)×int J is open, since compact C is closed in Euclidean space. There u is identically the fixed q by F001. At every point of this open set all positive-order coordinate derivatives vanish: the first derivative is the limit of zero difference quotients in a neighborhood, and repetition proves the assertion at every finite order. H002 gives continuity of all derivatives everywhere on the time-domain. Therefore, at any r∈int J, every spatial function listed in N002 is continuous and zero off C; its nonzero set has closure contained in the closed compact C. Apply M005 to each of these functions. This proves all their integrability claims, including e(·,t0), e_t and every product used later. Equivalently, each is bounded in absolute value on Q by M006 and has absolute integral at most that bound times (2R)^n.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"M005\",\"M006\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F003\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"t0\",\"J\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F004\",\"statement\":\"All positive-order derivatives of u vanish on (ℝ^n\\\\C)×int J. At every r∈int J the spatial sections of e, e_t, u_t·u_tt, ∂_{x_j}u·∂_{x_j}u_t, u_t·∂_{x_jx_j}u, u_t·∂_{x_j}u, and ∂_{x_j}(u_t·∂_{x_j}u) are continuous, supported in C and integrable, for each 1≤j≤n.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"time in int J\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"index 1≤j≤n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"C\",\"J\",\"r\",\"j\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S005\",\"text\":\"The closed time interval [3t0/4,5t0/4] lies in int J. The product Q×[3t0/4,5t0/4] is compact. H002 implies that e_t is continuous there, since e is a finite sum of squares of smooth component derivatives. Apply M006 to |e_t| on this space-time compact box to obtain a finite real upper bound.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"M006\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F003\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"Q\",\"J\",\"t0\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F005\",\"statement\":\"There exists B0∈ℝ with |e_t(x,r)|≤B0 for all (x,r)∈Q×[3t0/4,5t0/4].\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"B0\",\"type\":\"real\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of Q\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"time in [3t0/4,5t0/4]\"}],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"Q\",\"t0\",\"B0\",\"x\",\"r\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S006\",\"text\":\"Choose B0 as in F005 and put B=max(0,B0). Then B≥0 and the same bound holds with B. F003 and F004 give all the claimed support, measure and integrability assertions. In particular t0∈int J. Since t0 was arbitrary, e(·,t) is integrable at every positive time; the witnesses C,R,B establish the full more precise assertion.\",\"uses\":[],\"uses_local\":[\"F003\",\"F004\",\"F005\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"t0\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"J\",\"B0\",\"B\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"id\":\"A001\",\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"Y=ℝ^n, K=C, L=ℝ, f(x)=|x|.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℝ has its usual nonempty ordered topology with closed upper rays; ℝ^n has the Euclidean topology.\",\"C is compact by F001.\",\"The Euclidean norm is continuous, hence continuous on C.\"]},{\"id\":\"A002\",\"input\":\"M005\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝ^n, μ=Lebesgue measure, V=ℝ, f each spatial section in the displayed list, at any fixed r∈int J and index j.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The usual Euclidean Borel structure has measurable opens, and Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets; ℝ is a normed additive commutative group.\",\"H002 supplies continuous derivatives, and finite sums and products preserve continuity.\",\"The first part of S004 proves vanishing outside closed compact C, so each support closure is a closed subset of C and hence compact.\"]},{\"id\":\"A003\",\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"Y=ℝ^n, K=Q, L=ℝ, f the absolute value of each listed spatial function.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Standard real ordered and Euclidean topologies apply.\",\"F003 proves compactness of Q.\",\"H002 and the displayed component sums and products give continuity of these absolute values on Q.\"]},{\"id\":\"A004\",\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S005\",\"bindings\":\"Y=ℝ^n×ℝ, K=Q×[3t0/4,5t0/4], L=ℝ, f(x,r)=|e_t(x,r)|, extended arbitrarily outside the positive-time domain.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The source ordered codomain is the usual real line, with Euclidean product topology on Y.\",\"F003 gives compact Q and the time factor is a compact closed interval, so their finite product is compact.\",\"H002 gives continuity of e_t near this box, which lies at positive times; norm preserves continuity.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R003\",\"inputs\":[\"H002\",\"N001\",\"N002\",\"M001\",\"M004\",\"M009\"],\"output\":\"N003\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D003\",\"D004\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary t0>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose C from N002 at t0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose R together with C and B from N002 at t0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"C\"]},{\"symbol\":\"B\",\"type\":\"nonnegative real\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose B together with C,R from N002 at t0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"C\",\"R\"]},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"spatial box\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set Q=[−R,R]^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"η\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set η=t0/4.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"bnd\",\"type\":\"function ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Define bnd(x)=B 1_Q(x), the function equal to B on Q and zero elsewhere.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"B\",\"Q\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"F\",\"type\":\"function ℝ→ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"For 0<|h|<η define F(h,x)=(e(x,t0+h)−e(x,t0))/h; at other h set F(h,x)=0. Only the punctured germ at zero is used.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"e\",\"t0\",\"η\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix t0>0 and choose the compatible witnesses C,R,B from N002. Set Q=[−R,R]^n and η=t0/4. Then [t0−η,t0+η]=[3t0/4,5t0/4]⊂(t0/2,3t0/2). N002 proves |e_t|≤B on the corresponding space-time box. N001 gives that r↦e(x,r) is differentiable at each point of this interval with derivative e_t(x,r). For every x∈Q and 0<|h|<η, apply M004 on this real convex interval with endpoints t0,t0+h. It yields |e(x,t0+h)−e(x,t0)|≤B|h|. Outside Q both densities are zero by N002, since C⊂int Q. Therefore the absolute difference quotient is ≤B1_Q(x) for every x∈ℝ^n.\",\"uses\":[\"N001\",\"N002\",\"M004\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"t0\",\"C\",\"R\",\"B\",\"Q\",\"η\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"For all real h with 0<|h|<η and all x∈ℝ^n, |(e(x,t0+h)−e(x,t0))/h|≤B1_Q(x).\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"h\",\"type\":\"real\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"e\",\"t0\",\"η\",\"B\",\"Q\",\"h\",\"x\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Define bnd(x)=B1_Q(x). Q is closed and has finite measure (2R)^n by N002. M009 with constant B and set Q gives integrability of B on Q. Extending this restricted constant by zero off the measurable Q is exactly bnd, so bnd is integrable on ℝ^n.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M009\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"B\",\"Q\",\"R\",\"bnd\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"bnd(x)=B1_Q(x) is Lebesgue integrable on ℝ^n.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"B\",\"Q\",\"bnd\",\"x\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Define F(h,x) as the difference quotient for 0<|h|<η and zero otherwise. For these small nonzero h, its spatial section is continuous by N001 and is integrable by N002 and linearity of integrability. Hence it is a.e. strongly measurable. At every fixed x the difference quotients tend, as h→0 through h≠0, to e_t(x,t0), by N001. F001 and F002 give an integrable bound for the punctured germ. Apply M001 to Lebesgue measure on ℝ^n, scalar codomain ℝ, parameter h∈ℝ with filter 𝓝[≠]0, F as just defined, f(x)=e_t(x,t0), and bound bnd. The filter is countably generated, with the punctured intervals 0<|h|<1/k for positive integers k as a countable neighborhood basis. Consequently ∫F(h,x)dx→∫e_t(x,t0)dx along that filter.\",\"uses\":[\"N001\",\"N002\",\"M001\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"t0\",\"η\",\"F\",\"B\",\"Q\",\"bnd\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F003\",\"statement\":\"As h→0 with h≠0, ∫_{ℝ^n} F(h,x) dx tends to ∫_{ℝ^n} e_t(x,t0) dx.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"h\",\"type\":\"real\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"F\",\"e\",\"t0\",\"h\",\"x\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S004\",\"text\":\"N002 discharges the guard for E and gives integrability of e(·,t0+h) and e(·,t0) at the nearby positive times, as well as of e_t(·,t0). For 0<|h|<η, linearity of the integral gives ∫F(h,x)dx=(E(t0+h)−E(t0))/h. F003 is therefore exactly the difference-quotient criterion for differentiability of this finite real-valued E: E'(t0)=∫e_t(x,t0)dx. Substitute the full e_t identity from N001. Every summand is integrable by N002. H002 ensures the derivative notation has its usual classical meaning on the open time-domain. Since t0 was arbitrary, this proves N003.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"N001\",\"N002\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F003\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"E\",\"t0\",\"η\",\"F\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M004\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=V=ℝ, f(r)=e(x,r), S=[3t0/4,5t0/4], c=B, ξ=t0, ζ=t0+h, for any x∈Q and 0<|h|<η.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The scalar real structures satisfy the source's RCLike and normed-space conditions.\",\"N001 gives differentiability throughout this interval in the positive-time domain.\",\"N002 gives |e_t|≤B on Q×S, and N001 identifies this with the derivative of f.\",\"A real closed interval is convex, as follows directly from its two endpoint inequalities.\",\"η=t0/4 and 0<|h|<η place both t0 and t0+h in S.\"]},{\"input\":\"M009\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℝ^n with Lebesgue measure, S=Q, V=ℝ, c=B.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The Euclidean Lebesgue measurable space and ordinary real normed additive group supply these structures.\",\"N002 gives μ(Q)=(2R)^n<∞.\",\"B is an ordinary finite real number, so its extended norm is finite.\"]},{\"input\":\"M001\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℝ^n, μ=Lebesgue measure, G=ℝ, I=ℝ, l=𝓝[≠]0, F(h,x) as defined in S003, f(x)=e_t(x,t0), b=bnd.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The measurable-space, measure and scalar normed-space structures are the standard ones.\",\"The punctured real neighborhood filter has the displayed countable basis.\",\"For all sufficiently small nonzero h the section is continuous by N001, hence a.e. strongly measurable.\",\"F001 proves the bound everywhere for sufficiently small nonzero h.\",\"F002 proves integrability of bnd.\",\"N001 gives the pointwise derivative limit at every x; pointwise everywhere implies almost everywhere.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R004\",\"inputs\":[\"H002\",\"N002\",\"M002\",\"M003\",\"M007\"],\"output\":\"N004\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D003\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix t0>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"spatial index with 1≤j≤n\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix any index j with 1≤j≤n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose C from N002 at t0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose the compatible R from N002 at t0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"C\"]},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"spatial box\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Put Q=[−R,R]^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"R\"]},{\"symbol\":\"g\",\"type\":\"function ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Define g(x)=u_t(x,t0)·∂_{x_j}u(x,t0).\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"t0\",\"j\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix t0>0 and an index 1≤j≤n. Choose compatible C,R from N002 and put Q=[−R,R]^n and g(x)=u_t(x,t0)·∂_{x_j}u(x,t0). H002 makes g smooth. N002 gives g=0 off C and C⊂int Q, so g vanishes on a neighborhood of the boundary of Q (the open complement of C contains that boundary). Apply M007 along the jth coordinate to u_t and ∂_{x_j}u at time t0. This gives ∂_{x_j}g=∂_{x_j}u_t·∂_{x_j}u+u_t·∂_{x_jx_j}u. Each term is continuous and integrable by N002.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"N002\",\"M007\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"t0\",\"j\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"g\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"g is smooth, zero near ∂Q, and ∂_{x_j}g=∂_{x_j}u_t(·,t0)·∂_{x_j}u(·,t0)+u_t(·,t0)·∂_{x_jx_j}u(·,t0). All three functions in this equality are integrable and vanish outside C.\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"∂\",\"g\",\"Q\",\"C\",\"t0\",\"j\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"For each fixed choice of the remaining n−1 coordinates in [−R,R], consider the scalar function of x_j obtained by restricting g to that coordinate line. It is smooth on ℝ, including both endpoints −R and R. Its derivative is continuous and hence integrable on this compact interval, and the function has zero value at both endpoints because these points lie on ∂Q, outside C. M002 therefore gives ∫_{−R}^{R} ∂_{x_j}g dx_j=0. Use M003 with the jth restricted Lebesgue coordinate as the inner factor and the product of the other restricted coordinates as the outer factor. The integrand on Q is integrable by F001 (also continuous and bounded on a finite-measure box), and both factor measures are finite, hence s-finite. This yields ∫_Q ∂_{x_j}g dx=0. This product decomposition is just the standard coordinate product defining dx: regroup the finite coordinates and put the jth coordinate last; finite coordinate permutations preserve that product measure. If n=1 there are no remaining coordinates and no Fubini step is needed: the FTC equality already is the required box integral. Endpoints do not affect the Lebesgue interval integral since singletons have zero measure.\",\"uses\":[\"M002\",\"M003\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"t0\",\"j\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"g\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"∫_Q ∂_{x_j}g(x) dx=0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"∂\",\"Q\",\"g\",\"j\",\"x\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"All terms of F001 vanish outside Q by N002, since C⊂int Q. Thus their integrals over Q equal their whole-space integrals. Integrate the equality in F001 and use F002 and linearity for the integrable summands: 0=∫_{ℝ^n}∂_{x_j}u_t·∂_{x_j}u dx+∫_{ℝ^n}u_t·∂_{x_jx_j}u dx. Symmetry of the real dot product and subtraction give precisely the identity in N004, including finiteness of both sides. Since t0 and j were arbitrary this proves N004.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"t0\",\"j\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"g\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M007\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ, V=ℝ^m. Along the jth coordinate line use f=u_t(·,t0), g_source=∂_{x_j}u(·,t0), v=∂_{x_j}u_t and w=∂_{x_jx_j}u.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Standard Euclidean real inner-product structures apply.\",\"H002 supplies the spatial derivative of u_t.\",\"H002 supplies the spatial derivative of ∂_{x_j}u; addition and symmetry of the inner product give the order displayed in F001.\"]},{\"input\":\"M002\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"V=ℝ, α=−R, β=R, f the restriction of g to the jth coordinate line with remaining coordinates fixed in their box.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℝ is a complete normed additive commutative group and real normed space.\",\"F001 gives global spatial smoothness of g, hence differentiability of its line restriction at every point of [−R,R].\",\"The line derivative is continuous on the compact interval by F001, so is interval-integrable.\"]},{\"input\":\"M003\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"A=the other n−1 coordinates, B_source=ℝ for coordinate j, μ=product Lebesgue measure restricted to their box, ν=Lebesgue measure restricted to [−R,R], V=ℝ; f is ∂_{x_j}g under this coordinate regrouping (for n>1).\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The coordinate measurable structures and real normed-space structures apply. Both restricted product measures are finite, hence s-finite, by the finite lengths of these intervals.\",\"F001 proves integrability of ∂_{x_j}g, and its restriction to Q is integrable; under coordinate product regrouping this is exactly integrability for μ×ν.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R005\",\"inputs\":[\"H002\",\"N001\",\"N002\",\"N003\",\"N004\",\"M004\"],\"output\":\"P\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R005/energy cancellation\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S002\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix t0>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R005/constancy\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary a>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"positive real\",\"scope\":\"R005/constancy\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary b>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix t0>0. N002 proves integrability of e(·,t0) and all the products below and discharges the guard for the actual finite E. N003 gives differentiability and the integral of the full e_t identity. Sum the integration-by-parts identity N004 over the finite spatial indices and substitute it to obtain E'(t0)=2∫_{ℝ^n}u_t·u_tt dx−2∑_{j=1}^n ∫_{ℝ^n}u_t·∂_{x_jx_j}u dx=2∫_{ℝ^n}u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu) dx. Here H002 makes the component derivatives well-defined and D002 is precisely the finite componentwise sum. Integral linearity applies because N002 gives integrability of every summand.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"N002\",\"N003\",\"N004\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"Δ_xu\",\"e\",\"E\",\"t0\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"E is differentiable at t0 and E'(t0)=2∫_{ℝ^n} u_t(x,t0)·(u_tt(x,t0)−Δ_xu(x,t0)) dx, with finite integrals.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"Δ_xu\",\"E\",\"t0\",\"x\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"By N001 the integrand in F001 is identically zero, so its integral is zero and E'(t0)=0. The choice of t0 was arbitrary. Together with integrability from N002 and differentiability from N003 this proves all the requested pointwise-in-time assertions on (0,∞).\",\"uses\":[\"N001\",\"N002\",\"N003\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"Δ_xu\",\"e\",\"E\",\"t0\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"For every t>0, e(·,t) is integrable, E is finite and differentiable at t, and E'(t)=0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real\"}],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"E\",\"t\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"For the constancy asserted in the supplied proof, fix any a,b>0. On S=[min(a,b),max(a,b)] all times are positive. F002 gives differentiability of E at every point of S and |E'|≤0 there. Apply M004 over ℝ with c=0, ξ=a, ζ=b to get |E(b)−E(a)|≤0|b−a|=0, hence E(b)=E(a). This includes a=b. Substituting D003 and D004 shows the exact normalization in P, with no factor 1/2; all times used are strictly positive and no value or assertion at time zero was introduced.\",\"uses\":[\"M004\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"∂\",\"e\",\"E\",\"a\",\"b\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M004\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"K=V=ℝ, f=E on a neighborhood of the positive interval S=[min(a,b),max(a,b)] (extended arbitrarily elsewhere), c=0, ξ=a, ζ=b.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The ordinary real field and scalar normed-space structures apply.\",\"F002 gives differentiability at every time in S, all of which are positive.\",\"F002 gives E'=0 on S, so its absolute value is ≤0.\",\"The interval S is real-convex by the elementary interval inequalities.\",\"a and b are both in their unordered closed interval S.\"]}]}]}}}","status":"completed"}],"error":null,"incomplete_details":null,"tools":[{"type":"function","name":"search_mathlib","description":"在固定 mathlib 的 Lean 源码中搜索正则表达式，返回至多 100 行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_mathlib","description":"读取固定 mathlib 中一个 Mathlib/...lean 文件的指定行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start_line":{"type":"integer"},"line_count":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start_line","line_count"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"search_external_sources","description":"Follow source-retrieval.md: arxiv first (default; an exact arXiv ID is allowed), then google title/author search, then google title/author DOI search, then scihub with a confirmed DOI. Use browser:true for normal local-browser readback of JavaScript/verification pages. Results and DOI strings are locators, not source/theorem approval. Record failed access and continue conditionally when the original stays unavailable.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"},"engine":{"type":"string","enum":["arxiv","google","scihub"]},"browser":{"type":"boolean"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_external_source","description":"Read and retain a public HTTPS source and its hash-bound access record. Use offset to continue cached text. Retry with browser:true for a normal isolated local Chrome session if HTTPS needs JavaScript or verification. The optional browser uses ordinary site controls; unsupported challenges remain explicit failures. Verify full PDF identity, version, theorem and context yourself.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"url":{"type":"string"},"offset":{"type":"integer"},"limit":{"type":"integer"},"browser":{"type":"boolean"}},"required":["url"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"defer_primary_source","description":"After actual unavailable primary-source access, preserve a complete exact secondary quotation as an explicitly unverified external assumption. The statement must match the quote in a supplied input. Returns the verification record for E.source.verification; this never grants ordinary Audit A PASS.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"source_id":{"type":"string"},"statement":{"type":"string"},"input_id":{"type":"string"},"quote":{"type":"string"},"attempts":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"required":["source_id","statement","input_id","quote","attempts"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_task_input","description":"Read one declared frozen document. For JSON use pointer (empty string lists the root); large objects return keys, and strings resume with offset=next_offset. For text use inclusive start/end lines and column=next_column when continuing a long line. Every serialized result is at most 32 KiB. Follow has_more and the returned continuation coordinates; no arbitrary local files are accessible.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"},"column":{"type":"integer"},"pointer":{"type":"string"},"offset":{"type":"integer"},"limit":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["id"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_yui","description":"Read declared Yui rules/templates; no tests, historical runs or personal files.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"},"column":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start","end"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"check_artifact","description":"Check a JSON artifact against the explicit stage contract; this is not automatic mathematical certification.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact_json":{"type":"string"}},"required":["artifact_json"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"submit_artifact","description":"Submit a JSON artifact after its stage checks.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact_json":{"type":"string"}},"required":["artifact_json"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"report_obstacle","description":"Report only a real substantive/suspected mathematical GAP, unavailable source or conflicting original input, with all five located fields. Ordinary unfinished NL0, source registration and JSON bookkeeping are not reports: continue an actual lookup or draft edit. TASK_INCOMPLETE is unavailable for NL0 authoring. Difficulty or a long run is not evidence of a mathematical gap.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"status":{"type":"string","enum":["SUBSTANTIVE_GAP","SUSPECTED_GAP","SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE","MISSING_SOURCE_STATEMENT","INPUT_CONFLICT"]},"location":{"type":"string"},"explanation":{"type":"string"},"basis":{"type":"string"},"partial_progress":{"type":"string"}},"required":["status","location","explanation","basis","partial_progress"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"check_draft","description":"Preferred structured-object form of check_artifact: provide artifact as an actual JSON object, not an escaped JSON string. Same complete validation and acceptance scope.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true}},"required":["artifact"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"submit_draft","description":"Preferred structured-object form of submit_artifact: provide artifact as an actual JSON object, not an escaped JSON string. Same complete validation and acceptance scope.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true}},"required":["artifact"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"probe_library","description":"Query a proved Mathlib theorem and its exact signature/axioms in the offline sandbox. Returns located evidence for an M source; the mathematical statement and applicability still require review. The optional module locator uses dotted syntax (Mathlib.Topology.Basic), never a slash path; omit it if unknown.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"declaration":{"type":"string"},"module":{"type":"string"}},"required":["id","declaration"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"find_library_sources","description":"Search pinned foundational metadata by an exact declaration name or ordinary mathematical terms. Ranked lexical candidates are not a theorem whitelist or an applicability judgment. Selected sources still need explicit M roots and complete checks.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_draft","description":"Read a JSON pointer in your own same-stage candidate after an actual structural check. No other file/stage is accessible. For an existing string optionally supply both start_line/end_line to request up to 250 numbered lines and its complete text_sha256 for replace_lines. A read end beyond the text returns the remaining existing lines and actual range; the start must exist. Edits never clamp their range.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"candidate":{"type":"integer"},"pointer":{"type":"string"},"start_line":{"type":"integer"},"end_line":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["candidate","pointer"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_author_packet","description":"Same-author closing inspection of one structurally valid own conditional inference: exact direct inputs, output, declarations and own proof. No ancestor bodies, extra H, independent reviewer or Audit A feedback.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"candidate":{"type":"integer"},"inference":{"type":"string"}},"required":["candidate","inference"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"record_author_checks","description":"Save actual same-author notes and readiness against shared Audit A criteria. Unresolved/not-checked partial notes can be saved, but only complete actual packet inspection with no known issue can release a proof. Open issues persist until explicit located resolutions. No PASS or mathematical verdict is generated; independent Au